---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Verbosity - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="fundamentals" subChapterId="verbosity">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				AI models are verbose by default. Ask a simple question, get a five-paragraph essay. Request
				a code fix, receive a tutorial on the entire framework. This is a
				consequence of how these models were trained. They learned from text written by humans trying
				to be thorough and provide tutorial-level details, and they absorbed that tendency at scale.
			</p>

			<p>
				The problem is that verbosity costs you time. The AI can read and write at enormous speed,
				but you can't. Every unnecessary paragraph it generates is something you have to read,
				evaluate, and either use or discard. In a fast-moving workflow, especially when you're
				iterating on code or working through requirements, excess output becomes a real bottleneck.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Taming the Output
			</h3>

			<p>
				The good news is that models respond well to explicit instructions about format and length.
				You don't have to accept the default verbosity. A few practical approaches:
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<Callout variant="tip" title="State the format you want">
					<p>
						"Give me just the code, no explanation." "Answer in one sentence." "Bullet points
						only." These work remarkably well. The model will follow formatting instructions if you
						give them.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Set the persona">
					<p>
						"You're a senior developer talking to another senior developer", or "talk to me as an equal" changes the output
						significantly. The model will skip the basics and get to the point. Establishing your
						expertise level early prevents unnecessary tutorials.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Use system prompts and tool configs">
					<p>
						Both Cursor and Claude Code let you configure persistent instructions (via an AGENTS.md
						file, rules files, or CLAUDE.md). Adding "be concise" or "skip explanations unless asked" to
						these configs saves you from repeating it every conversation.
					</p>
				</Callout>

				<Callout variant="tip" title="Ask for revisions">
					<p>
						If you get a wall of text, just say "shorter" or "just the key points." Models are good
						at condensing their own output on request. This is sometimes faster than crafting the
						perfect prompt upfront.
					</p>
				</Callout>
			</div>

			<h3>
				How Far People Take This
			</h3>

			<p>
				Verbosity is a common enough frustration that people have built skills specifically to fight
				it. Two well-known examples: <a href="https://github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-orange-600 underline hover:text-orange-700">Caveman</a>, which forces the
				model into stripped-down, telegraphic prose (no pleasantries, no hedging, short declarative
				sentences) and can cut token usage sharply, and <a href="https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-orange-600 underline hover:text-orange-700">Ponytail</a>,
				which pushes the model to write the minimum code necessary rather than over-building. They
				target two different flavors of verbosity: chatty prose and over-engineered code.
			</p>

			<p>
				These are useful to know about, but they're a bit extreme for everyday work. Caveman output can
				be a chore for a human to read, and aggressive code minimalism can strip out things you
				actually wanted. For day-to-day use, the plain instructions above (state the format, set the
				persona, ask for revisions) get you most of the benefit without the rough edges.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="info" title="A hint from leaked reasoning">
				<p>
					There's some indirect evidence that terse thinking really is more efficient. Both OpenAI and
					Anthropic hide their models' raw reasoning by default, so you usually only see a cleaned-up
					summary, but every so often the tooling{" "}
					<a href="https://finance.biggo.com/news/160e94937690998d" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-orange-600 underline hover:text-orange-700">leaks the real thing</a>.
					When it has, OpenAI's models in particular appear to reason in a clipped, almost
					caveman-style shorthand, nicknamed "grug brain" after the cult-classic{" "}
					<a href="https://grugbrain.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-orange-600 underline hover:text-orange-700">Grug Brained Developer</a>{" "}
					essay: fragments like "Need absolute path. Need know cwd." rather than full sentences. Treat this as anecdote, not
					fact. A leaked or summarized trace isn't a reliable window into what the model is actually
					doing. But it's a fun hint that the instinct behind Caveman isn't baseless.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				When Verbosity is Useful
			</h3>

			<p>
				Verbosity isn't always bad. When you're learning something new, exploring an unfamiliar
				codebase, or trying to understand why something works a certain way, detailed explanations
				are exactly what you want. The skill is knowing when to ask for depth and when to ask for
				brevity. As a rule of thumb: exploration benefits from verbosity, execution benefits from
				conciseness.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="The practical takeaway">
				<p>
					Don't accept the default verbosity as inevitable. Tell the model what format you want, how
					much detail you need, and what to skip. You'll save significant time over the course of a
					day, and the output you get will be far more useful. This becomes especially important in
					the prompt engineering chapter, where we'll cover how to structure your requests for
					maximum signal and minimum noise.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
